WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.340
What do a mouthful of live moths, an army of&nbsp;
cats, and a crazy Bette Midler fan have in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:09.540
common? "Hocus Pocus" might not be the&nbsp;
cult classic it is today without them.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.000
Although today he's one of the top actors in&nbsp;
Hollywood, Leonardo DiCaprio was young and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:21.120
unproven in the early 1990s. His first official&nbsp;
movie credit came via "Critters 3" in 1991,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.780
and he starred alongside Robert&nbsp;
De Niro in his next feature film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:28.500
1993's "This Boy's Life." According to him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.700
the actor then had to make a difficult choice&nbsp;
as to which film he wanted to appear in next.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:37.980
In a 2014 interview with Variety, DiCaprio&nbsp;
recalled being offered "more money than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.880
[he] ever dreamed of" to star in "Hocus&nbsp;
Pocus." Instead of accepting, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.120
he opted to hold out for a role&nbsp;
in "What's Eating Gilbert Grape,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.020
a movie he hadn't even auditioned&nbsp;
for yet. The Oscar winner admitted,

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.860
"I don't know where the hell I got the nerve.&nbsp;
But if there's one thing I'm very proud of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.920
it's being a young man who&nbsp;
was sticking to my guns."

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.720
DiCaprio had auditioned for the part of Max&nbsp;
Dennison, which eventually went to "Dallas" actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:05.580
Omri Katz. While Katz gave a fine performance&nbsp;
as the film's leading man, you have to wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:10.743
just how different "Hocus Pocus" would have looked&nbsp;
with a future A-lister like DiCaprio in the role.

00:01:11.092 --> 00:01:16.020
"I'm the king of the world. Wooo!"

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:20.640
Eagle-eyed fans on Reddit have noticed that&nbsp;
in the Halloween party scene of "Hocus Pocus,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:25.020
there's someone in the background wearing&nbsp;
what appears to be an outfit from 1982's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.280
"Tron." As it turns out, that man's costume&nbsp;
is more authentic than you might realize.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:34.200
When speaking to Glamour, Mary Vogt, the&nbsp;
costume designer for "Hocus Pocus," admitted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:38.100
that she spent almost all the wardrobe&nbsp;
budget creating the iconic looks of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.660
Sanderson sisters and the zombified Billy&nbsp;
Butcherson. This made for a particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:46.680
sticky situation when the script called&nbsp;
for the Dennisons to attend a crowded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:50.820
Halloween party toward the end of the film&nbsp;
and Vogt "had completely run out of money."

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:55.860
So what did she do? Well, she took advantage of&nbsp;
the fact that she was working on a Disney movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:59.460
raiding the Walt Disney costume&nbsp;
department and selecting outfits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:03.420
from a slew of old Disney movies. The&nbsp;
Emmy-nominated costume designer recalled,

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:07.320
"I think that if you paid $5,000,&nbsp;
you could have whatever you want.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.740
So I went through there and took&nbsp;
everything that looked like a costume."

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:14.640
No wonder so many of those Halloween&nbsp;
outfits look so impressively genuine.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:19.140
There's a scene in "Hocus Pocus"&nbsp;
when the zombified Billy Butcherson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:22.980
finally catches up to Max and the&nbsp;
gang — but then, in a plot twist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:27.240
sides with the children against his ex-lovers.&nbsp;
You might recall that during this scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:32.040
Billy cuts open his sewn-up mouth and several&nbsp;
moths fly out. Being that this was the '90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:36.900
the stunt was performed without the assistance&nbsp;
of CGI. But you have to imagine that Doug Jones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.980
the veteran physical maestro who brought&nbsp;
Butcherson to life, wishes there was.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:46.140
In an interview with Bloody Disgusting, Tony&nbsp;
Gardner, the makeup FX master behind "Hocus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:50.280
Pocus," shared some photos and facts about&nbsp;
the film's behind the scenes happenings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:54.240
including that Jones actually had real&nbsp;
moths in his mouth during that scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:58.680
Gardner explained that his team attached&nbsp;
a latex pocket between the actor's upper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:03.300
and lower dentures, completely blocking&nbsp;
his throat ... and then came the moths.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:06.060
"An animal wrangler would place&nbsp;
several moths in the pocket with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:09.960
tweezers, under the supervision of a&nbsp;
representative from the Humane Society."

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:15.120
Jones himself vividly recalls the experience,&nbsp;
telling E! News that he could feel the moths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.320
fluttering around inside his mouth. Making&nbsp;
matters worse, a light burst on set while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:24.060
filming the first attempt, so Jones had&nbsp;
to redo the entire process. In retrospect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:28.892
Jones probably deserves an Academy Award&nbsp;
for enduring the moths not once, but twice.

00:03:28.892 --> 00:03:32.100
There's a joke Jimmy Kimmel said&nbsp;
about a strict policy that he has:

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:35.340
"I will not and do not publicize unsubstantiated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:37.980
rumors about anyone ...&nbsp;
unless they're very funny."

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:41.820
This must have been the mindset that&nbsp;
Doug Jones had when he publicly shared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:46.320
a pretty wild rumor about his legendary&nbsp;
costar, Bette Midler, telling E! News,

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.380
"I heard rumor that she had written&nbsp;
into her contract that there had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:52.140
be an isolated shot of her feet&nbsp;
walking somewhere in the film,"

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:57.480
While anonymous rumors are obviously not a solid&nbsp;
source of information, there are actually two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:01.920
very suspicious shots of Midler's feet during&nbsp;
the Halloween party scene that certainly lend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.840
fuel to the fire — one when she enters the room,&nbsp;
and another when she's walking across the stage.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:10.080
While it could simply be a&nbsp;
coincidence, Jones isn't buying.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:14.136
"Why would they even bother to put that&nbsp;
clip in there? I think it was contractual."

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:19.680
Sometimes it takes a good movie several years to&nbsp;
become fully appreciated. There's a plethora of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:24.540
solid films that bombed at the box office because&nbsp;
they were arguably ahead of their time, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:29.760
cult classics like "Labyrinth," "Heathers" and,&nbsp;
of course, "Blade Runner," which famously only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:35.940
took home $26 million on a reported budget of&nbsp;
$28 million, but is now recognized as one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:40.740
the best sci-fi flicks of all time. "Hocus Pocus"&nbsp;
achieved a similar type of delayed appreciation.

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:45.180
When speaking to Forbes in 2020,&nbsp;
director Kenny Ortega opened up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:50.400
about how poorly the film did at the&nbsp;
box office, only grossing $39.5 million.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.340
"Honestly, at that point, I&nbsp;
thought it was all over for me."

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:58.500
However, history had other plans for the&nbsp;
director and his little film that could. "Hocus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:03.360
Pocus" slowly but surely landed with its intended&nbsp;
audience, evolving into something truly special.

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:07.620
Looking back, Ortega is grateful for&nbsp;
the example the movie set in his life.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:12.300
"What I've learned is don't give up. I believed&nbsp;
in 'Hocus Pocus' ... and even though it wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:16.200
found by audiences immediately, the&nbsp;
generations have believed in it."

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:21.240
They say don't meet your heroes, but Kathy&nbsp;
Najimy would beg to differ. Before she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:25.560
landed the role of Mary Sanderson, Najimy was&nbsp;
already familiar with her on-screen sister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:28.620
Bette Midler. However, the&nbsp;
two had never actually met.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:34.080
During a 2019 interview with Metro Weekly, the&nbsp;
"Veep" actress explained that she had been a,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:36.900
quote, "crazy sycophant&nbsp;
fan" of Midler's growing up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:41.260
so getting cast to play her sister in&nbsp;
"Hocus Pocus" was a dream come true.

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:45.640
"When I was growing up the idol&nbsp;
of my life was Bette Midler and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:48.780
I used to break into backstages&nbsp;
of where she was performing."

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:51.540
In addition to sneaking backstage at her shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:56.160
Najimy also admitted to one audacious&nbsp;
occasion in which she posed as a worker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:00.480
for a singing telegram company and sang&nbsp;
Midler a song while wearing a bunny suit.

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:04.620
When asked whether she ever told Midler that&nbsp;
she was the bunny suit singer from her past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:09.120
Najimy says she did come clean, but it probably&nbsp;
took Midler a while to come to terms with it.

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:11.880
"I think it was just a very&nbsp;
slow sort of like, 'Oh God,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:16.103
I'm making a movie with a crazy person,'&nbsp;
into very good castmates and friends."

00:06:16.103 --> 00:06:20.940
While "Hocus Pocus" is obviously a fictional&nbsp;
tale, the roots of witchcraft in early America&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:26.640
are anything but. The Salem Witch Trials of&nbsp;
1692 are some of the most fascinating events&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:32.220
in American history, albeit a grisly example of&nbsp;
how intense paranoia can lead to mass chaos. Over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:37.500
the course of seven months, over 200 people in&nbsp;
the Puritan town of Salem, Massachusetts were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:41.940
accused of witchcraft, ultimately resulting&nbsp;
in twenty innocent people being murdered.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:47.340
One such person accused of witchcraft was named&nbsp;
Esther Dutch Elwell, who, as fate would have it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:52.440
is actually the tenth-great grandma to actress&nbsp;
Sarah Jessica Parker, the ditzy witch Sarah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:57.720
Sanderson in "Hocus Pocus." The "Sex and the City"&nbsp;
star learned of this stunning relation in 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:02.220
while appearing on the American version of the&nbsp;
British TV series "Who Do You Think You Are?"

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:06.300
Fortunately for Elwell, the trial court&nbsp;
for witchcraft was dissolved a few weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:10.080
before her hearing, so she was freed&nbsp;
and able to live a full life. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:12.840
the fact that one of her distant&nbsp;
relatives would eventually play a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:17.226
witch from roughly that same time period&nbsp;
is a rather fortuitous coincidence.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:21.420
Although "Hocus Pocus" is clearly a&nbsp;
movie intended for the Halloween season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:25.140
it actually hit theaters in July.&nbsp;
This means that filming for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:29.220
movie actually took place around Christmas&nbsp;
time, which made things a little tricky.

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:33.420
While some of "Hocus Pocus" was filmed&nbsp;
at studios in California, some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:38.640
production did actually take place in Salem,&nbsp;
Massachusetts for authenticity. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:42.540
when the crew traveled to Salem, people were&nbsp;
starting to put up their Christmas ornaments.

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:46.620
While holiday cheer is great, this&nbsp;
was for the wrong holiday. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:51.780
action had to be taken, so the filmmakers got out&nbsp;
their checkbooks and began approaching the locals.

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:54.300
Actor Tobias Jelinek told E! News,

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:56.640
"They were having to pay them out, block by block,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:00.450
to take down all their decorations so&nbsp;
we could get all the Halloween stuff."

00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:04.140
One of the scenes shot in LA&nbsp;
is the one in which Max, Dani,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:08.400
and Allison celebrate having just bested&nbsp;
the witches by dancing around in a park&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:12.600
outside the school. Although there's nothing&nbsp;
particularly special about this short scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:16.200
internet sleuths have discovered an&nbsp;
unexpected cameo in the background.

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:20.520
As it turns out, this scene was filmed&nbsp;
at the old Warner Bros. ranch in Burbank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:25.380
the exact same location where the iconic intro&nbsp;
for "Friends" was shot. You can clearly see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:30.524
the fountain that Joey, Rachel and the gang play&nbsp;
around in behind Dani as she prances in the park.

00:08:30.767 --> 00:08:35.520
As entertaining as "Hocus Pocus" is, the journey&nbsp;
of the screenplay behind the movie is arguably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:40.500
even more interesting. At the 2018 Midsummer&nbsp;
Scream, some of the cast and crew members from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:46.320
Disney's fan-favorite Halloween flick reunited for&nbsp;
the 25th anniversary of the film's release. One of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:50.400
the members on the panel was David Kirschner,&nbsp;
the man credited with creating the story.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:53.880
Kirschner explained that it started&nbsp;
as a bedtime story he would tell to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:58.080
his daughters. He eventually wrote it&nbsp;
as a short story and it got published&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:03.060
in Muppet Magazine. Then, in 1986, the&nbsp;
story was transformed into a screenplay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:08.220
where it would sit in limbo for eight years. When&nbsp;
Kirschner finally did sell the story to Disney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:13.260
he reportedly brought in loads of Halloween&nbsp;
decorations, including a giant bag chock full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:17.859
of candy corn so that the executives "smelled&nbsp;
their childhood" while he gave his pitch.

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:23.820
Thackery Binx, a young boy from the late 1600s,&nbsp;
was cursed by Winifred Sanderson to live forever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:28.800
in the form of a black cat. Binx is one of&nbsp;
the high points of the movie, periodically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:33.473
cracking jokes at Max's expense while watching&nbsp;
over Dani as though she was his own sister.

00:09:33.473 --> 00:09:37.936
"Take good care of Dani, Max. You'll&nbsp;
never know how precious she is."

00:09:37.936 --> 00:09:42.000
According to Humane Hollywood, there were&nbsp;
several different cats that played Binx&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:46.140
throughout the movie, and the handlers chose&nbsp;
which cat would appear in which scene based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:50.160
on their skills and personalities. American&nbsp;
Humane was present throughout production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:54.240
ensuring that no animals were&nbsp;
harmed during filming. It's truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:57.180
impressive what the crew was able&nbsp;
to accomplish with animal actors.

